---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import GetSalvage from "../../components/GetSalvage.astro";
import PluginHold from "../../components/PluginHold.astro";
import { REFIT_SOUNDING_HREF } from "../../lib/refit-sounding";
import scanEngine from "../../examples/refit-scan-engine.txt?raw";
import scanPsd from "../../examples/refit-scan-psd.txt?raw";
import patch from "../../examples/refit-patch.txt?raw";
import scanPatched from "../../examples/refit-scan-patched.txt?raw";
import buildTrap from "../../examples/refit-build-trap.txt?raw";
import buildOk from "../../examples/refit-doc-build.txt?raw";
import undo from "../../examples/refit-undo.txt?raw";

const captured =
  "Real output on an Apple M4 Mac with Unreal Engine 5.8.4. The project path is shortened.";

const verdicts: [string, string, string][] = [
  ["works on Mac", "Mac binaries, and nothing in the way.", "Nothing to do."],
  ["content only, works on Mac", "No code, only assets.", "Nothing to do."],
  ["works on Mac without its Windows-only modules", "Its Windows-only modules are left out on a Mac, and the rest work.", "Nothing to do."],
  ["allow list only: patch (Mac binaries are there)", "The Mac binaries ship, but the plugin's list of platforms leaves Mac out.", "patch"],
  ["allow list only: patch, then rebuild", "Lists leave Mac out, there are no Mac binaries, and the scan found nothing else in the way.", "patch, then build"],
  ["rebuild for Mac", "Nothing keeps Mac out, but there are no Mac binaries.", "build"],
  ["needs porting", "Windows API calls, Windows-only libraries or Windows-only build rules.", "A person ports what's listed, then patch and build."],
  ["no source: ask the vendor", "Only binaries. Without source there's nothing to rebuild.", "Ask the vendor for a Mac build or the source."],
];
---
<Page
  title="Rebuild a Windows-only Unreal plugin for Mac"
  compact
  description="Can you run Windows Unreal plugins on a Mac? Not as they are, but you can often rebuild them. How to use salvage refit to scan a plugin, patch its allow lists, build it for Mac and check that the build really produced Mac binaries."
  dek="salvage refit tells you why a plugin won't work on a Mac, lets Mac through its allow lists on a copy in your project, builds it, and checks the build really made Mac binaries. Porting code stays with a person."
>
  <div class="prose wide">
    <h2>Can I run a Windows plugin on my Mac?</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Not as it is. You can often rebuild it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A Windows plugin's binaries are <code>.dll</code> files: Windows' own format, built for Intel and AMD processors,
      calling Windows' APIs. The Mac editor is a different kind of program, for Apple silicon (and Intel), on a
      different operating system. There's no way to load one inside the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      What you can do is build the plugin again, for Mac, from its source. Often the only thing in the way is a list in
      the plugin that names the platforms it's for, and Mac isn't on it. Sometimes the code calls Windows directly, or
      leans on a library that only comes for Windows, and then someone has to port it. Across all 894 plugins that ship
      with Unreal Engine 5.8.4, here's how that split:
    </p>
  </div>

  <PluginHold />

  <div class="prose wide">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A Mac set up to build Unreal C++:</strong> Xcode with the Metal Toolchain. <a
        href="/charts/salvage-doctor/"><code>salvage doctor</code></a> checks both.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Unreal Engine.</strong> Refit has been tried with 5.8.4, Epic's binary build.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>The plugin's source.</strong> Its <code>Source</code> folder, not only its binaries. Refit says when a
        plugin has none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A project to put the rebuilt plugin in.</strong> A Blueprint project is fine. Refit never changes the
        engine install, so it works on a copy there.
      </li>
      <li><strong>The <code>salvage</code> command.</strong> See <a href="#get-salvage">Get it</a> below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>1. Scan</h2>
    <p>
      In a project's folder, <code>salvage refit scan</code> checks every plugin in its <code>Plugins</code> folder.
      Add <code>--engine-plugins</code> for the engine's own, including Fab plugins installed into the engine:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit scan --engine-plugins" output={scanEngine} caption="Real output on an Apple M4 Mac with Unreal Engine 5.8.4, in 6.5 seconds. It goes on to list every plugin that doesn't simply work, 126 here." />
    <p>
      Give it one plugin's folder and it shows everything in that plugin's way, with files and lines. Here's Epic's
      PSDImporter, which imports layered Photoshop files and ships for Windows only:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit scan Engine/Plugins/Experimental/PSDImporter" output={scanPsd} caption={captured} />
    <p>
      <code>--details</code> shows that for every plugin in a bigger scan, <code>--all</code> lists the ones that already
      work too, and <code>--json</code> prints it all for scripts.
    </p>

    <h3>What the verdicts mean</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="verdicts">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Verdict</th><th scope="col">Why</th><th scope="col">Next</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {verdicts.map(([v, why, next]) => (
            <tr><td><code>{v}</code></td><td>{why}</td><td>{next}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The scan reads the <code>.uplugin</code>, the Mac binaries, every <code>Build.cs</code>, prebuilt libraries, and
      the C and C++ sources. In the sources it skips comments and strings, follows <code>#if</code> and its relatives,
      and reports Windows headers, Win32 calls and Win32 types outside any Windows-only block. These are heuristics:
      they catch the usual reasons a plugin won't build on a Mac, not every one. "Nothing else in the way" doesn't
      promise the plugin is useful on a Mac, or that it builds without surprises.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Patch</h2>
    <p>
      <code>salvage refit patch</code> adds Mac to the plugin's <code>SupportedTargetPlatforms</code> and to each
      module's <code>PlatformAllowList</code>. A plugin from outside the project, the engine for one, is copied into the
      project's <code>Plugins</code> folder first:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit patch &quot;/Users/Shared/Epic Games/UE_5.8/Engine/Plugins/Experimental/PSDImporter&quot;" output={patch} caption={captured} />
    <p>
      It changes only those lists, in place, keeping the file's layout, after backing up the <code>.uplugin</code>.
      Run it again and it reports nothing to change. <code>--dry-run</code> shows what it would do first.
    </p>
    <p>
      A patch doesn't make a plugin portable. PSDImporter still needs its library after one:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit scan Plugins/PSDImporter" output={scanPatched} caption={captured} />

    <h2>Porting, if it needs it</h2>
    <p>
      If the scan says <code>needs porting</code>, this is where a person takes over. Refit lists what's in the way and
      where; building a third-party library for Mac, or changing code that calls Windows, is up to whoever does the
      port. For PSDImporter that meant building its PSD library for Mac and fixing two crashes, one in the plugin and
      one in the library. <a href={REFIT_SOUNDING_HREF}>The sounding</a> logs every step.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Build</h2>
    <p>
      <code>salvage refit build</code> runs Unreal's BuildPlugin for Mac, then checks the result. That check is the
      point. A plugin whose lists leave Mac out "builds" on a Mac in under a second, prints <code>BUILD
      SUCCESSFUL</code>, and compiles nothing. Here's the unpatched PSDImporter:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit build ~/Projects/Refitted/Plugins/PSDImporter" output={buildTrap} caption={`${captured} The whole command took 0.75 seconds; BuildPlugin's own log said BUILD SUCCESSFUL.`} />
    <p>
      After the patch and the port, the same command took 2 minutes 37 seconds and compiled 124 files for Apple silicon
      and Intel. The check confirms that every module the editor loads has a Mac binary, and that each one is a real
      Mach-O file exporting Unreal's module marker:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit build Plugins/PSDImporter --install" output={buildOk} caption="An excerpt from the sounding: the check at the end of the build." />
    <p>
      The package goes to <code>Saved/Refit/&lt;Plugin&gt;</code> in the project, or wherever <code>--out</code> says. <code>--install</code> copies the Mac binaries into the plugin's folder, so the project's editor loads them. <code>--engine</code> picks the engine to build with; by default it's the project's, then the newest installed.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Check</h2>
    <p>
      <code>salvage refit check</code> runs the same check on a package that's already built, without building again.
      Pass BuildPlugin's log with <code>--log</code> to also check that files were compiled and that it built for a
      platform at all. It's useful for a package someone else built, or in a script:
    </p>
    <pre><code>salvage refit check Saved/Refit/PSDImporter --log Saved/Refit/PSDImporter.log</code></pre>
    <p><code>build</code> and <code>check</code> take <code>--json</code> too.</p>

    <h2>Undo it</h2>
    <p>
      <code>salvage refit patch --undo</code> puts the <code>.uplugin</code> back byte for byte. If refit made the copy
      and nothing but build output changed in it, the copy goes too:
    </p>
    <Terminal command="salvage refit patch Plugins/PSDImporter --undo" output={undo} caption={captured} />

    <h2>What refit doesn't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Port code.</strong> It tells you what's in the way and where. Building a library, replacing a Windows
        call or fixing what breaks on a Mac is a person's job, and it can take a few tries: PSDImporter crashed twice
        before it imported a file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run Windows binaries.</strong> Nothing does that for the Unreal editor. Without source, there's
        nothing for refit to rebuild.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prove a plugin works.</strong> A clean build means it compiled into real Mac binaries. Whether it loads,
        and whether every feature behaves on a Mac, is something to try.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Change the engine install.</strong> Patches go on a copy in your project.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide what you can share.</strong> A rebuilt plugin is still under its own license. Check it before
        you pass the Mac binaries on.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it's been tried</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        On Unreal Engine 5.8.4 on an Apple M4 Mac: a scan of all 894 engine plugins, and PSDImporter rebuilt and
        importing a PSD in the editor. <a href={REFIT_SOUNDING_HREF}>The sounding</a> has the details, and what wasn't
        tried.
      </li>
      <li>
        Not on Fab plugins. They show up in the engine scan when they're installed into the engine, but none were
        installed on this Mac.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you rebuild a plugin with it, an <a href="https://github.com/Chapterists/salvage/issues">issue</a> saying what
      worked and what didn't would help the next person.
    </p>

    <GetSalvage />
  </div>
</Page>
<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: var(--s-5);
  }
  .verdicts {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 36rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  .verdicts th,
  .verdicts td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4) var(--s-3) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .verdicts thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--text);
  }
  .verdicts td:first-child {
    width: 34%;
  }
  .verdicts td:last-child {
    width: 24%;
  }
  .note {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--mark);
    padding-left: var(--s-4);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  .prose code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
